    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "48 hours seems like a massive amount of time just to update a single record. is there something actually happening in the background during that window, or is it just servers waiting for a timer to expire? like, what determines how fast it moves?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "During propagation, DNS resolvers periodically check the authoritative server for updates based on Time-To-Live (TTL) settings. The TTL, which you set when publishing the record, tells resolvers how long they are allowed to cache the old information before checking again. A lower TTL means servers check more frequently, speeding up the update once it's made.",
